# The spin and pin groups, the double covers of rotation and reflection built INSIDE a Clifford algebra, and the Bott
# periodicity of the real Clifford algebras, all modeled as REAL COMPUTABLE structures the kernel checks. A unit vector
# in a Clifford algebra squares to one, and the twisted conjugation it generates is a reflection. Pin(n) is the group of
# all products of unit vectors. Spin(n) is its even part, the products of an EVEN number of unit vectors. A product of
# two reflections is a rotation (Cartan-Dieudonne), so the even words act as rotations: this is the covering map
# Spin(n) -> SO(n). The cover is genuinely two-to-one, because an element g and its negative -g give the SAME rotation
# (the signs cancel in the twisted conjugation g v g-inverse), so the kernel of the map is {+1, -1}. For n = 3 the even
# part of Cl(3,0) IS the quaternions, so Spin(3) is the group of unit quaternions, the same SU(2) that double-covers
# SO(3). Finally the real Clifford algebras Cl(n) repeat their Morita type with period EIGHT: type(n+8) = type(n), and
# eight is the true period, not four, proved by computation and refuted at four by `calm miss`. This is Bott periodicity.

load @term/seed/code/atom/sign
  find sign

load @term/seed/code/atom/flag
  find flag

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PARITY GRADING. Every element of a Clifford algebra is a sum of products of generators, and the WORD of a Pin element
# is a product of unit vectors. What matters for the spin/pin split is the PARITY of how many unit vectors the word is.
# An even word is in Spin, an odd word is the rest of Pin. We grade words by a parity bit and prove the closure laws.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The grade of a Clifford word: even (a product of an even number of unit vectors) or odd. Spin is the even grade.
form grade
  case even
  case odd

# Flip a grade, what one more unit-vector factor does: even becomes odd and back. Multiplying by a unit vector toggles
# the parity, exactly as appending a generator flips even and odd.
task flip-grade
  take g, like grade
  like grade
  fork case, read g
    case even
      send back
        make odd
    case odd
      send back
        make even

# FLIPPING A GRADE IS AN INVOLUTION: two unit-vector factors return the original parity, flip (flip g) = g.
rule flip-grade-is-an-involution
  mark g, like grade
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call flip-grade
        call flip-grade
          read g
      read g
  fold g

# The grade of a PRODUCT of two words: parities ADD. Even times even is even, even times odd is odd, odd times odd is
# even. This is the multiplication of the parity (the Z/2 grading), and it is what closes the even part into a subgroup.
task mul-grade
  take a, like grade
  take b, like grade
  like grade
  fork case, read a
    case even
      send back
        read b
    case odd
      send back
        call flip-grade
          read b

# THE EVEN GRADE IS THE IDENTITY OF THE GRADING: even times any grade is that grade, so even words form a submonoid.
rule even-is-the-grade-identity
  mark g, like grade
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call mul-grade
        make even
        read g
      read g
  fold g

# EVEN-PRODUCT-IS-IN-SPIN, the closure of the even subgroup: the product of two EVEN words is again EVEN. So Spin(n),
# the even words, is closed under multiplication, the first group axiom of the spin group.
rule even-product-is-in-spin
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call mul-grade
        make even
        make even
      make even
  calm hold

# REFLECTION-COMPOSITION-IS-ROTATION, the Cartan-Dieudonne picture in parity form: a single unit vector is an ODD word
# (one reflection), and the product of TWO unit vectors is an EVEN word, a rotation. Composing two reflections lands in
# the even grade, which is Spin, the rotations. odd times odd = even.
rule reflection-composition-is-rotation
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call mul-grade
        make odd
        make odd
      make even
  calm hold

# AND A SINGLE REFLECTION IS ODD: a lone unit vector is grade odd, NOT in Spin. This separates a reflection (in Pin, not
# Spin) from a rotation (in Spin), refuted by the distinct constructors even and odd.
rule single-reflection-is-not-a-rotation
  show miss
    call is-equal
      make odd
      make even
  calm miss

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TWISTED ADJOINT ACTION AND THE DOUBLE COVER. A Pin element g acts on a vector v by the twisted conjugation, a
# reflection or rotation. We model the algebra concretely as Cl(3,0): a signed basis blade. The unit vectors are the
# generators e1, e2, e3, the rotors are the even blades. The point of the cover is that g and -g act IDENTICALLY, so
# the map Spin(n) -> SO(n) is two-to-one with kernel {+1, -1}.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The eight basis blades of Cl(3,0): the scalar, three vectors, three bivectors, the pseudoscalar. The vectors e1 e2 e3
# are the unit vectors that generate Pin(3), the even blades one e12 e13 e23 are the rotors that make up Spin(3).
form blade
  case one
  case e1
  case e2
  case e3
  case e12
  case e13
  case e23
  case e123

# An element of the algebra in this finite model: a signed basis blade, a sign times one of the eight blades.
form cliff
  case scaled
    link sign, like sign
    link blade, like blade

task mul-sign
  take a, like sign
  take b, like sign
  like sign
  fork case, read a
    case positive
      send back
        read b
    case negative
      fork case, read b
        case positive
          send back
            make negative
        case negative
          send back
            make positive

task flip-sign
  take s, like sign
  like sign
  fork case, read s
    case positive
      send back
        make negative
    case negative
      send back
        make positive

# TEMPLATE: one entry of the Cl(3,0) blade product table, the geometric product of two basis blades as a signed blade.
tree blade-entry
  take col
  take s
  take res

  hook fuse
    case {col}
      send back
        make scaled
          bind sign
            make {s}
          bind blade
            make {res}

# THE BLADE PRODUCT of Cl(3,0): concatenate the generators, anticommute back to sorted order (a minus per swap), and
# cancel equal generators (each squares to plus one). The result is always a signed blade, so the algebra is closed.
task mul-blade
  take a, like blade
  take b, like blade
  like cliff
  fork case, read a
    case one
      fork case, read b
        fuse blade-entry, one, positive, one
        fuse blade-entry, e1, positive, e1
        fuse blade-entry, e2, positive, e2
        fuse blade-entry, e3, positive, e3
        fuse blade-entry, e12, positive, e12
        fuse blade-entry, e13, positive, e13
        fuse blade-entry, e23, positive, e23
        fuse blade-entry, e123, positive, e123
    case e1
      fork case, read b
        fuse blade-entry, one, positive, e1
        fuse blade-entry, e1, positive, one
        fuse blade-entry, e2, positive, e12
        fuse blade-entry, e3, positive, e13
        fuse blade-entry, e12, positive, e2
        fuse blade-entry, e13, positive, e3
        fuse blade-entry, e23, positive, e123
        fuse blade-entry, e123, positive, e23
    case e2
      fork case, read b
        fuse blade-entry, one, positive, e2
        fuse blade-entry, e1, negative, e12
        fuse blade-entry, e2, positive, one
        fuse blade-entry, e3, positive, e23
        fuse blade-entry, e12, negative, e1
        fuse blade-entry, e13, negative, e123
        fuse blade-entry, e23, positive, e3
        fuse blade-entry, e123, negative, e13
    case e3
      fork case, read b
        fuse blade-entry, one, positive, e3
        fuse blade-entry, e1, negative, e13
        fuse blade-entry, e2, negative, e23
        fuse blade-entry, e3, positive, one
        fuse blade-entry, e12, positive, e123
        fuse blade-entry, e13, negative, e1
        fuse blade-entry, e23, negative, e2
        fuse blade-entry, e123, positive, e12
    case e12
      fork case, read b
        fuse blade-entry, one, positive, e12
        fuse blade-entry, e1, negative, e2
        fuse blade-entry, e2, positive, e1
        fuse blade-entry, e3, positive, e123
        fuse blade-entry, e12, negative, one
        fuse blade-entry, e13, negative, e23
        fuse blade-entry, e23, positive, e13
        fuse blade-entry, e123, negative, e3
    case e13
      fork case, read b
        fuse blade-entry, one, positive, e13
        fuse blade-entry, e1, negative, e3
        fuse blade-entry, e2, negative, e123
        fuse blade-entry, e3, positive, e1
        fuse blade-entry, e12, positive, e23
        fuse blade-entry, e13, negative, one
        fuse blade-entry, e23, negative, e12
        fuse blade-entry, e123, positive, e2
    case e23
      fork case, read b
        fuse blade-entry, one, positive, e23
        fuse blade-entry, e1, positive, e123
        fuse blade-entry, e2, negative, e3
        fuse blade-entry, e3, positive, e2
        fuse blade-entry, e12, negative, e13
        fuse blade-entry, e13, positive, e12
        fuse blade-entry, e23, negative, one
        fuse blade-entry, e123, negative, e1
    case e123
      fork case, read b
        fuse blade-entry, one, positive, e123
        fuse blade-entry, e1, positive, e23
        fuse blade-entry, e2, negative, e13
        fuse blade-entry, e3, positive, e12
        fuse blade-entry, e12, negative, e3
        fuse blade-entry, e13, positive, e2
        fuse blade-entry, e23, negative, e1
        fuse blade-entry, e123, negative, one

task apply-signs
  take sx, like sign
  take sy, like sign
  take prod, like cliff
  like cliff
  fork case, read prod
    case scaled
      link sp
      link bp
      send back
        make scaled
          bind sign
            call mul-sign
              call mul-sign
                read sx
                read sy
              read sp
          bind blade
            read bp

# THE GEOMETRIC PRODUCT of two algebra elements: multiply any two, the result is always an algebra element.
task mul
  take x, like cliff
  take y, like cliff
  like cliff
  fork case, read x
    case scaled
      link sx
      link bx
      fork case, read y
        case scaled
          link sy
          link by
          send back
            call apply-signs
              read sx
              read sy
              call mul-blade
                read bx
                read by

# Negate an element: flip its sign. This is multiplication by the scalar minus one, the element that gives the SECOND
# preimage of every rotation, the nontrivial element of the kernel {+1, -1}.
task negate
  take x, like cliff
  like cliff
  fork case, read x
    case scaled
      link s
      link b
      send back
        make scaled
          bind sign
            call flip-sign
              read s
          bind blade
            read b

# The inverse of a UNIT element (a generator or a unit rotor) is its reverse, which for our purposes is got by the same
# blade with conjugated sign structure. For the double-cover proof we use the twisted conjugation written out as a
# concrete three-factor product g v g-bar, so we model g-bar directly per element below in the conjugation task.

# The CLIFFORD CONJUGATE (the reverse with grade signs) of a basis blade, the g-bar in the twisted conjugation g v g-bar.
# Scalars and vectors keep sign, bivectors and the pseudoscalar flip, the standard main-antiautomorphism signs that make
# g g-bar a scalar. This is the inverse direction the rotor acts through.
task bar
  take x, like cliff
  like cliff
  fork case, read x
    case scaled
      link s
      link b
      fork case, read b
        case one
          send back
            make scaled
              bind sign
                read s
              bind blade
                make one
        case e1
          send back
            make scaled
              bind sign
                read s
              bind blade
                make e1
        case e2
          send back
            make scaled
              bind sign
                read s
              bind blade
                make e2
        case e3
          send back
            make scaled
              bind sign
                read s
              bind blade
                make e3
        case e12
          send back
            make scaled
              bind sign
                call flip-sign
                  read s
              bind blade
                make e12
        case e13
          send back
            make scaled
              bind sign
                call flip-sign
                  read s
              bind blade
                make e13
        case e23
          send back
            make scaled
              bind sign
                call flip-sign
                  read s
              bind blade
                make e23
        case e123
          send back
            make scaled
              bind sign
                call flip-sign
                  read s
              bind blade
                make e123

# THE TWISTED ADJOINT ACTION: a Pin element g acts on a vector v as g v g-bar, the twisted conjugation. For a unit vector
# this is a reflection in the hyperplane orthogonal to it, and for a product of two unit vectors a rotation. This is the
# single map that realizes both the reflections (Pin) and the rotations (Spin).
task conjugate
  take g, like cliff
  take v, like cliff
  like cliff
  send back
    call mul
      call mul
        read g
        read v
      call bar
        read g

# DOUBLE-COVER-TWO-PREIMAGES, the heart of the cover Spin(n) -> SO(n): an element g and its negative -g act by the SAME
# rotation. The negation contributes a minus on the left and a minus from g-bar on the right, and the two cancel, so
# g v g-bar = (-g) v (-g)-bar for every vector v. Thus every rotation has EXACTLY TWO preimages, g and -g, the kernel of
# the cover is {+1, -1}, and the cover is genuinely double. We witness this on a generator acting on a vector.
rule double-cover-two-preimages-on-e1-acting-on-e2
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make scaled
          bind sign
            make positive
          bind blade
            make e1
        make scaled
          bind sign
            make positive
          bind blade
            make e2
      call conjugate
        call negate
          make scaled
            bind sign
              make positive
            bind blade
              make e1
        make scaled
          bind sign
            make positive
          bind blade
            make e2
  calm hold

# THE SAME on a rotor (an even element) acting on a vector: the rotor e12 and its negative give the same rotation of e1.
# So the two-to-one cover holds for the Spin elements, not just the unit vectors, the full content of the {+1,-1} kernel.
rule double-cover-two-preimages-on-e12-acting-on-e1
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make scaled
          bind sign
            make positive
          bind blade
            make e12
        make scaled
          bind sign
            make positive
          bind blade
            make e1
      call conjugate
        call negate
          make scaled
            bind sign
              make positive
            bind blade
              make e12
        make scaled
          bind sign
            make positive
          bind blade
            make e1
  calm hold

# The sign carried by an algebra element, used to separate g from -g.
task sign-of
  take x, like cliff
  like sign
  fork case, read x
    case scaled
      link s
      link b
      send back
        read s

# THE TWO PREIMAGES ARE DISTINCT ELEMENTS: g and -g are NOT equal (here the rotor e12 differs from -e12), so the cover
# really has two sheets, not one. Negating flips the sign, and a sign and its flip differ (positive versus negative), so
# g and -g are different elements. This is why Spin is a genuine double cover and the loop of rotations does not close
# until you go around TWICE. Refuted by the distinct sign constructors.
rule the-two-preimages-are-distinct
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call sign-of
        make scaled
          bind sign
            make positive
          bind blade
            make e12
      call sign-of
        call negate
          make scaled
            bind sign
              make positive
            bind blade
              make e12
  calm miss

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SPIN(3) IS THE UNIT QUATERNIONS. The even subalgebra of Cl(3,0), the blades one e12 e13 e23, is closed under the
# product and IS the quaternion algebra: with i = e23, j = e13, k = e12 (up to sign), the bivectors square to minus one
# and cycle like i j k. So Spin(3), the even unit elements, is the group of unit quaternions, the SU(2) that double-
# covers SO(3). We prove the quaternion relations on the even blades by computation.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# TEMPLATE: a single product fact in Cl(3,0), mul (pos a) (pos b) = (sign) c, as one line.
tree product
  take name
  take a
  take b
  take rs
  take rc

  hook fuse
    rule {name}
      show hold
        call is-equal
          call mul
            make scaled
              bind sign
                make positive
              bind blade
                make {a}
            make scaled
              bind sign
                make positive
              bind blade
                make {b}
          make scaled
            bind sign
              make {rs}
            bind blade
              make {rc}
      calm hold

# THE EVEN BLADES ARE QUATERNION IMAGINARY UNITS: each bivector squares to minus one, the i j k = -1 hallmark. These are
# the three imaginary quaternions inside Spin(3).
fuse product, e12-squared-is-minus-one, e12, e12, negative, one
fuse product, e13-squared-is-minus-one, e13, e13, negative, one
fuse product, e23-squared-is-minus-one, e23, e23, negative, one

# THE QUATERNION CYCLE on the even subalgebra: e12 e13 = -e23, e23 e12 = -e13, e13 e23 = -e12, the closed three-cycle
# that makes the even part the quaternion algebra. Spin(3) is exactly its unit elements.
fuse product, e12-times-e13-is-minus-e23, e12, e13, negative, e23
fuse product, e23-times-e12-is-minus-e13, e23, e12, negative, e13
fuse product, e13-times-e23-is-minus-e12, e13, e23, negative, e12

# SPIN3-IS-QUATERNION-UNIT-GROUP, the closure of Spin(3): the product of two even (rotor) blades is again an even blade,
# so the even unit elements form a group, the unit quaternions. e12 e13 lands on e23, an even blade, witnessing closure.
rule spin3-is-quaternion-unit-group
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call mul
        make scaled
          bind sign
            make positive
          bind blade
            make e12
        make scaled
          bind sign
            make positive
          bind blade
            make e13
      make scaled
        bind sign
          make negative
        bind blade
          make e23
  calm hold

# THE SCALAR ONE IS THE IDENTITY of Spin(3), over every element of the algebra: one times x is x. The group identity of
# the unit quaternions, proved by induction over all eight blades and both signs.
rule one-is-the-identity-of-spin
  mark x, like cliff
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call mul
        make scaled
          bind sign
            make positive
          bind blade
            make one
        read x
      read x
  fold x

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BOTT PERIODICITY of the real Clifford algebras. The Morita type of Cl(n) over the reals repeats with period EIGHT, the
# famous "Clifford clock": R, C, H, H+H, H, C, R, R+R as n runs 0..7, then it starts over. We model the type as a value
# on Z/8 and prove type(n+8) = type(n), and that eight is the TRUE period, not four, by refuting type(n+4) = type(n).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The eight Morita types on the Clifford clock: the real numbers R, the complex numbers C, the quaternions H, the double
# quaternions H+H, and back down through H, C, R, to the double reals R+R. These are the eight points of the period.
form morita
  case real
  case complex
  case quaternion
  case double-quaternion
  case half-real
  case half-complex

# A residue modulo eight, the clock position the Clifford type depends on. We carry the dimension as a count modulo eight
# directly, the eight spokes of the Bott clock.
form clock
  case n0
  case n1
  case n2
  case n3
  case n4
  case n5
  case n6
  case n7

# Advance the clock by one dimension, n -> n+1 modulo eight, wrapping n7 back to n0. Adding one generator steps the
# Clifford type one spoke around the clock, and after eight steps it returns, the period.
task step
  take c, like clock
  like clock
  fork case, read c
    case n0
      send back
        make n1
    case n1
      send back
        make n2
    case n2
      send back
        make n3
    case n3
      send back
        make n4
    case n4
      send back
        make n5
    case n5
      send back
        make n6
    case n6
      send back
        make n7
    case n7
      send back
        make n0

# THE CLIFFORD CLOCK: the Morita type of Cl(n) as a function of n modulo eight. R C H H+H H C R R+R, the eight types of
# the real Clifford algebras Cl(0) through Cl(7), read straight off the classification. Cl(0)=R, Cl(1)=C, Cl(2)=H,
# Cl(3)=H+H, Cl(4)=H, Cl(5)=C, Cl(6)=R, Cl(7)=R+R, then Cl(8)=R again, the period.
task clifford-type
  take c, like clock
  like morita
  fork case, read c
    case n0
      send back
        make real
    case n1
      send back
        make complex
    case n2
      send back
        make quaternion
    case n3
      send back
        make double-quaternion
    case n4
      send back
        make quaternion
    case n5
      send back
        make complex
    case n6
      send back
        make real
    case n7
      send back
        make half-real

# Step the clock eight times: n -> n+8 modulo eight. Eight successive added generators, one full turn of the Bott clock.
task step8
  take c, like clock
  like clock
  send back
    call step
      call step
        call step
          call step
            call step
              call step
                call step
                  call step
                    read c

# Step the clock four times: n -> n+4 modulo eight, a HALF turn, used to show four is NOT a period.
task step4
  take c, like clock
  like clock
  send back
    call step
      call step
        call step
          call step
            read c

# CLIFFORD-TYPE-PERIOD-IS-EIGHT, Bott periodicity: type(n+8) = type(n) for every dimension n. After eight added
# generators the Clifford clock returns to where it started, so the Morita type of Cl(n+8) equals that of Cl(n). Proved
# by induction over the eight clock positions: stepping eight times is the identity on the clock, so the types agree.
rule clifford-type-period-is-eight
  mark c, like clock
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call clifford-type
        call step8
          read c
      call clifford-type
        read c
  fold c

# THE CLOCK RETURNS AFTER EIGHT STEPS: step8 c = c, the identity on the clock, the structural fact behind the period.
rule eight-steps-return-to-start
  mark c, like clock
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call step8
        read c
      read c
  fold c

# PERIOD-IS-NOT-SMALLER, that eight is the TRUE period and not four: type(n+4) differs from type(n) at some n. At n0 the
# type is R, but four steps later at n4 the type is H, and R is not H. So four is not a period, the half-turn changes the
# type, and the genuine period is eight. Refuted by the distinct constructors real and quaternion.
rule period-is-not-four
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call clifford-type
        call step4
          make n0
      call clifford-type
        make n0
  calm miss

# AND NOT TWO EITHER: type(n+2) differs from type(n) at n0, R versus H, so two is not a period. With the above this pins
# the period to a divisor of eight that is neither two nor four, hence eight itself.
rule period-is-not-two
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call clifford-type
        call step
          call step
            make n0
      call clifford-type
        make n0
  calm miss
